WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.445
Many embedded Rust projects use `no_std`,
limited memory, fixed buffers,

2
00:00:04.525 --> 00:00:07.406
static allocation, or carefully controlled
heap use.

3
00:00:07.486 --> 00:00:09.740
These constraints shape security design.

4
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.014
A firmware component may have no operating
system services,

5
00:00:13.094 --> 00:00:15.598
limited diagnostics, strict timing
behavior,

6
00:00:15.678 --> 00:00:19.373
and little room for unbounded allocation
or panic-oriented failure.

7
00:00:19.453 --> 00:00:23.085
Rust's ownership and borrowing rules help
clarify who owns buffers,

8
00:00:23.165 --> 00:00:24.981
peripherals, handles, and state.

9
00:00:25.061 --> 00:00:29.444
That clarity can prevent many classes of
memory corruption and aliasing mistakes.

10
00:00:29.524 --> 00:00:34.096
In embedded systems, ownership clarity
also helps reviewers understand when a buffer

11
00:00:34.176 --> 00:00:36.743
can be reused, when a peripheral is
initialized,

12
00:00:36.823 --> 00:00:39.829
and which component is responsible for
cleanup or reset.

13
00:00:39.909 --> 00:00:43.290
Safe Rust does not automatically solve
every resource problem.

14
00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:47.128
Stack usage, buffer sizes, static storage
layout, allocator choices,

15
00:00:47.208 --> 00:00:50.464
integer conversions, watchdog timing,
maximum frame sizes,

16
00:00:50.544 --> 00:00:53.550
and worst-case input behavior still need
design review.

17
00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:57.637
A memory-safe program can still exhaust
memory, miss timing expectations,

18
00:00:57.717 --> 00:00:59.847
or fail poorly under resource pressure.

19
00:00:59.927 --> 00:01:03.934
Defensive embedded Rust favors bounded
data structures, explicit limits,

20
00:01:04.014 --> 00:01:07.208
predictable failure behavior, and small
trusted surfaces.

21
00:01:07.288 --> 00:01:09.730
When a heap is used, its policy should be
clear.

22
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.943
When static buffers are used, their
ownership and maximum use should be clear.

23
00:01:14.023 --> 00:01:16.653
When conversion or arithmetic determines a
size,

24
00:01:16.733 --> 00:01:18.674
the failure path should be explicit.

25
00:01:18.754 --> 00:01:21.698
The practical question is what happens
under pressure.

26
00:01:21.778 --> 00:01:25.535
If input is larger than expected, if a
queue fills, if a timer fires late,

27
00:01:25.615 --> 00:01:28.996
if parsing cannot complete, or if a
peripheral fails to respond,

28
00:01:29.076 --> 00:01:33.397
the firmware should fail in a controlled
way that matches the product's safety and

29
00:01:33.477 --> 00:01:34.980
reliability requirements.
